# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Merge behaviour for GENERATED files.
#
# docs/offline-reliability-sync.json is written by
# scripts/offline-reliability-sync.js (`npm run offline:record`), never by
# hand. It stores a SHA-256 over the CONTENTS of the watched source files
# (194 at the time of writing), and CI recomputes that hash and fails the
# build if it disagrees — .github/workflows/ci.yml → `npm run check:offline`.
#
# Because the pre-commit hook refreshes it on both sides of a
# HotFix → QA → Production promotion, it conflicted on essentially every
# cross-branch merge. This resolves it silently by taking the INCOMING copy.
#
# ─── Why "theirs" and not "ours" ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The recorded hash is a function of the merged SOURCE tree, so the only
# correct value is the one matching the code that ends up on the branch.
# Promotion here is one-directional — HotFix is always ahead of QA and
# Production (verified 2026-08-13: neither carries a commit HotFix lacks) —
# so the incoming side's hash is the one that matches the incoming source.
#
# `merge=ours` would do the opposite: pin the target branch's OLD hash on top
# of the incoming NEW source. `check:offline` would then mismatch by
# construction, converting a 10-second merge conflict into a red CI run on
# QA/Production — quieter at merge time and strictly worse.
#
# ─── ⚠ REQUIRED ONE-TIME SETUP, PER CLONE ───────────────────────────────────
#
# The attribute alone does NOTHING. Git ships no built-in "theirs" merge
# driver, and merge drivers are deliberately never configured from a tracked
# file (they run arbitrary commands, so a repo cannot grant itself execution
# on clone). Each developer runs, once:
#
#     git config merge.theirs.driver 'cp -- %B %A'
#
# Without it the file simply conflicts exactly as it does today — a safe
# failure mode, not a broken merge. CI never merges, so CI needs no setup.
#
# ─── When neither side is right ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# If a merge ever spans genuinely divergent source (both branches carrying
# watched-file commits the other lacks), no parent's hash is correct — the
# merged tree is a new tree. Regenerate after the merge instead:
#
#     npm run offline:record:worktree
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
docs/offline-reliability-sync.json merge=theirs
